1588,632],"years":[2024,2025]},"where_people_are":{"text":"The United States hosts {p0} of all Sierra Leonean asylum seekers (2025), down from {p1} in 2000","pcts":[7.6,7.9],"years":[2025,2000]}},"steps":{"brief":true,"key_numbers":true,"over_time":true,"where_people_are":true,"inside_outside":true,"compare":false,"context":true,"other_angles":true,"limitations":true,"sources":true},"brief_sources":[],"footnotes":{"fn_1440d2f1":"The United States of America has also offered temporary status in certain circumstances to people in need of international protection. UNHCR is in discussions with the U.S. government about how to best reflect such datapoints for all relevant populations in future reporting cycles.","fn_1a948537":"Asylum applications which are reported as cases can be multiplied overall by average number of person per case of 1.501 when summarising the data by country of asylum, but not origin.  This is due to the significant variance in the average case size by country of origin.","fn_1f28097e":"An improved methodology to estimate the number of refugees hosted in the United States of America, using official statistics from US Citizenship and Immigration Services, led to a decrease of the refugee stock within the country.","fn_27837164":"The United States of America has not adopted a formal status determination procedure to identify stateless persons, and there is no official government data-tracking of the stateless population in the USA. As such, while the USA has a stateless population, the actual number is unknown.","fn_3264335f":"The United States of America has not adopted a formal status determination procedure to identify stateless persons, and there is no official government data-tracking of the stateless population in the USA. As such, while the USA has a stateless population, the actual number is unknown.","fn_41286920":"The United States of America has also offered temporary status in certain circumstances to people in need of international protection. UNHCR is in discussions with the U.S. government about how to best reflect such datapoints for all relevant populations in future reporting cycles.","fn_5bd885eb":"All statistics reported by the United States of America apart from resettlement arrivals are as of mid-2024. Hence, the data of new applications and decisions only include the period between January and June 2024, and the number of pending applications is as of 30 June 2024. The full year statistics for each of these are likely to be higher.","fn_6d41dffb":"The United States of America has also offered temporary status in certain circumstances to people in need of international protection. UNHCR is in discussions with the U.S. government about how to best reflect such datapoints for all relevant populations in future reporting cycles.","fn_6d97c150":"Statistics for the United States of America in 2025 are based on official data published by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services (USCIS) and the Executive Office for Immigration Review (EOIR). EOIR data covers the full year. USCIS data is reported as cases from January to September 2025, on this basis UNHCR counts one person per case. USCIS data does not include the applicants’ nationality for new asylum applications.","fn_75096460":"The United States of America has not adopted a formal status determination procedure to identify stateless persons, and there is no official government data-tracking of the stateless population in the USA. As such, while the USA has a stateless population, the actual number is unknown.","fn_76325ced":"The refugee figure is currently under review, which may lead to an adjustment in future reports.","fn_7d527b1a":"UNHCR’s global reporting standard for asylum statistics is for individual level data. To meet this standard, US Citizenship and Immigration Services (USCIS) shared the 2021 asylum data expressed in terms of individuals. Previously, USCIS provided data based on asylum “cases” and average family household size, which UNHCR used to estimate the total number of individual asylum applications before USCIS. The shift in 2021 to USCIS reporting of individuals (not cases) allows for more precise reporting, as well as more consistency across the U.S. asylum data, since the U.S. Executive Office for Immigration Review (EOIR) has already been reporting individual-level asylum statistics to UNHCR. UNHCR notes that the increase in the reported number of asylum applications filed with USCIS in 2021 primarily reflects this change in reporting modality.","fn_7e1062d3":"The United States of America has also offered temporary status in certain circumstances to people in need of international protection. For example, in 2022, a significant number of Ukrainians and their immediate family members were given temporary protection on a humanitarian basis in the United States. UNHCR is in discussions with the U.S. government about how to best reflect such datapoints for all relevant populations in future reporting cycles.","fn_7e948401":"Asylum applications and pending figures are adjusted with the average number of person per case(1.46).","fn_8c129950":"The United States of America has not adopted a formal status determination procedure to identify stateless persons, and there is no official government data-tracking of the stateless population in the USA. As such, while the USA has a stateless population, the actual number is unknown.","fn_96866b00":"Asylum applications which are reported as cases can be multiplied overall by average number of person per case of 1.501 when summarising the data by country of asylum, but not origin.  This is due to the significant variance in the average case size by country of origin.","fn_9bc17ca7":"Asylum applications which are reported as cases can be multiplied overall by average number of person per case of 1.501 when summarising the data by country of asylum, but not origin.  This is due to the significant variance in the average case size by country of origin.","fn_a40cf541":"The United States of America has not adopted a formal status determination procedure to identify stateless persons, and there is no official government data-tracking of the stateless population in the USA. As such, while the USA has a stateless population, the actual number is unknown.","fn_abab6808":"Asylum applications which are reported as cases can be multiplied overall by average number of person per case of 1.5 when summarising the data by country of asylum, but not origin.  This is due to the significant variance in the average case size by country of origin.","fn_ac56b6f9":"The United States of America has not adopted a formal status determination procedure to identify stateless persons, and there is no official government data-tracking of the stateless population in the USA. As such, while the USA has a stateless population, the actual number is unknown.","fn_b563440a":"The United States of America has not adopted a formal status determination procedure to identify stateless persons, and there is no official government data-tracking of the stateless population in the USA. As such, while the USA has a stateless population, the actual number is unknown.","fn_dcf5c20b":"The United States of America has not adopted a formal status determination procedure to identify stateless persons, and there is no official government data-tracking of the stateless population in the USA. As such, while the USA has a stateless population, the actual number is unknown.","fn_de669593":"All statistics reported by the United States of America apart from resettlement arrivals are as of mid-2024. Hence, the data of new applications and decisions only include the period between January and June 2024, and the number of pending applications is as of 30 June 2024. The full year statistics for each of these are likely to be higher.","fn_ee15b47d":"The United States of America has not adopted a formal status determination procedure to identify stateless persons, and there is no official government data-tracking of the stateless population in the USA. As such, while the USA has a stateless population, the actual number is unknown.","fn_f697e626":"The United States of America has also offered temporary status in certain circumstances to people in need of international protection. For example, in 2022, a significant number of Ukrainians and their immediate family members were given temporary protection on a humanitarian basis in the United States. UNHCR is in discussions with the U.S. government about how to best reflect such datapoints for all relevant populations in future reporting cycles."},"raw_files":{"conflict.csv":{"label":"IDMC GIDD, conflict and violence (HDX csv)","header":["iso3","country_name","year","new_displacement","new_displacement_rounded","total_displacement","total_displacement_rounded"]},"persons_of_concern.csv":{"label":"UNHCR Refugee Data Finder download (persons_of_concern.csv)","header":["Year","Country of Asylum","Country of Origin","Country of Asylum ISO","Country of Origin ISO","Refugees","Asylum-seekers","IDPs","Other people in need of international protection","Stateless persons","Host community","Others of concern"]}},"raw_rows":{"conflict.csv#L564":{"file_key":"conflict.csv","line":564,"row":["SLE","Sierra Leone","2018","3000","3000","3000","3000"]},"conflict.csv#L624":{"file_key":"conflict.csv","line":624,"row":["SLE","Sierra Leone","2019","2500","2500","5500","5500"]},"conflict.csv#L686":{"file_key":"conflict.csv","line":686,"row":["SLE","Sierra Leone","2020","","","2500","2500"]},"conflict.csv#L750":{"file_key":"conflict.csv","line":750,"row":["SLE","Sierra Leone","2021","","","2500","2500"]},"conflict.csv#L815":{"file_key":"conflict.csv","line":815,"row":["SLE","Sierra Leone","2022","","","0",""]},"conflict.csv#L878":{"file_key":"conflict.csv","line":878,"row":["SLE","Sierra Leone","2023","","","0",""]},"conflict.csv#L946":{"file_key":"conflict.csv","line":946,"row":["SLE","Sierra 